{"qas": [
  {"question": "According to arXiv 2602.13244, what are two main benefits for companies of implementing Explainable AI, and how does the EU AI Act reinforce its importance?", "answer": "Explainable AI improves trust and reduces liability risks for companies, and the EU AI Act explicitly demands transparency, making explainability unavoidable.", "evidence": [{"page": 11, "quote": "For companies, this means better decision-making foundations, lower liability risks, and greater trust in AI-supported processes."}, {"page": 10, "quote": "Transparency, in particular, is explicitly demanded by the EU AI Act (see Chapter 2) and thus becomes an unavoidable topic for the use of AI systems."}]},
  {"question": "In arXiv 2602.13244, how does the EU AI Act address both data protection and transparency obligations for companies using AI systems?", "answer": "The EU AI Act applies alongside GDPR for data protection, and imposes transparency obligations such as informing users when interacting with AI and labeling synthetic media.", "evidence": [{"page": 7, "quote": "The EU AI Act complements existing regulatory frameworks and applies without prejudice to data protection requirements. As soon as AI systems process personal data, the GDPR remains fully applicable."}, {"page": 8, "quote": "Transparency obligations: Users must be able to recognize when they are interacting with an AI (unless this is obvious anyway, e. g. an animated game character). Anyone who creates and publishes synthetic media (deepfakes, AI-generated images/videos/texts with potential to deceive) must label this, although there is flexibility regarding how visible such labeling must be."}]},
  {"question": "According to arXiv 2602.13244, what is the definition of 'deployer' under the EU AI Act, and what is a typical borderline case for provider classification?", "answer": "A deployer is a company that licenses or purchases AI software for internal use without modifying or marketing it, while self-hosting an LLM for employees without external distribution is a borderline provider case.", "evidence": [{"page": 6, "quote": "- Deployer: Do you purchase or license AI software (e. g. a chatbot or automation software) and use it within your company without modifying the system yourself or marketing it under your own name? In that case, you are a deployer."}, {"page": 7, "quote": "A typical borderline case in practice is the local operation of an AI model (self-hosting). If, for example, you operate a large language model (LLM) on your own servers and make it available to your employees, without making any substantial modifications and without distributing the system externally, you are generally also to be regarded as a provider."}]},
  {"question": "In arXiv 2602.13244, what does 'LoRA' stand for and how does it relate to model compression?", "answer": "LoRA stands for Low-Rank Adaptation, a technique that adapts only a subset of model parameters, enabling efficient fine-tuning and is related to low-rank decomposition in model compression.", "evidence": [{"page": 17, "quote": "A modern and efficient technique for this is Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA). In contrast to classical fine-tuning, only a subset of the model parameters typically individual layers is adapted, which enables fast and resource-efficient fine-tuning of large models."}, {"page": 18, "quote": "- Low-rank decomposition: Decomposition of complex weight matrices into smaller components (cf. LoRA in Section 4.2) [29],[33]. Low-rank decomposition and related methods such as LoRA are particularly useful when a shared base model is to be retained for many use cases, while adaptations are implemented in the form of lightweight adapters that can be trained quickly and deployed in a resource-efficient manner."}]},
  {"question": "According to arXiv 2602.13244, what is a key challenge of continuous learning in language models?", "answer": "A major challenge is catastrophic forgetting, where previously learned content is overwritten when new data is integrated.", "evidence": [{"page": 17, "quote": "A major challenge in this context is so-called catastrophic forgetting (CF), in which earlier learned content is overwritten as soon as new data is integrated [31]."}]},
  {"question": "In arXiv 2602.13244, what is the main advantage of using model compression for AI deployment on edge devices?", "answer": "Model compression reduces memory and computational requirements, enabling efficient AI deployment on resource-constrained edge devices without significant loss of performance.", "evidence": [{"page": 18, "quote": "The goal is to make AI models efficiently usable even in resource-constrained environments such as mobile devices or edge computing, which also makes model compression a key enabling technology for the democratization of AI."}]}
]}
